\cf7 11\cf11 4. **Reference matching** \uc0\u8594  find the `<ref>` entry in the citer whose `<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid|doi">` matches the cited source, giving its reference id (`rid`).\
\cf7 12\cf11 5. **Sentence extraction** \uc0\u8594  find every sentence containing an `<xref>` pointing to that `rid`, extract it verbatim.\
\cf7 13\cf11 6. **Fabrication guard** \uc0\u8594  each extracted sentence is confirmed as a verbatim substring of the citer's whitespace-normalized full text (100% pass by construction; independently re-verified on a sample).\
\cf7 14\cf11 \
\cf7 15\cf11 **Key consequence:** claim\uc0\u8594 cited-source linkage is now **metadata-grounded** (via the JATS `rid`/`pub-id` match), not inferred from proximity. This directly serves the project rule to prefer structured record data over free-text inference.\
\cf7 16\cf11 \
\cf7 17\cf11 ## Yield (3 pilot sources, one per category)\
\cf7 18\cf11 \
\cf7 19\cf11 | Source | Category | PMC citers examined | Ref matched | Verbatim claim extracted |\
\cf7 20\cf11 |---|---|---|---|---|\
\cf7 21\cf11 | Wakefield MMR/autism (PMID 9500320) | A retracted | 13 | 5 (38%) | 3 |\
\cf7 22\cf11 | Resveratrol in mice (PMID 17086191) | B species | 15 | 12 (80%) | 8 |\
\cf7 23\cf11 | Pembrolizumab melanoma trial (PMID 25891173) | C control | 15 | 14 (93%) | 8 |\
\cf7 24\cf11 | **TOTAL** | | **43** | **31 (72%)** | **19 (44%)** |\
\cf7 25\cf11 \
\cf7 26\cf11 **Bottom line: 44% of examined PMC citers yield a usable verbatim claim.** With 13\'9646 PMC-indexed citers available per source (before examination) and 5 candidate sources staged per category, hitting 5\'967 verified cases per category is comfortable. **Yield is NOT a constraint.**\
\cf7 27\cf11 \
\cf7 28\cf11 ## Two qualitative findings that shape case selection (need your awareness)\
\cf7 29\cf11 \
\cf7 30\cf11 **1. Category A \'97 many citers explicitly flag the retraction.** For the Wakefield paper, several citing sentences already say "fraudulent research," "now retracted study," "debunked claim." Ground truth ("the cited paper is retracted") holds regardless, but the *useful* stress-test for a verification tool is an **uncritical citation** that treats the retracted finding as valid. Both exist in the citer pool. **Plan: prefer neutral/uncritical citing claims where available, and label each A case with whether the citer acknowledged the retraction** (an extra `citer_acknowledges_retraction` field) \'97 this is itself useful eval metadata. Wakefield may be a poor choice precisely because it's so infamous; lower-profile retractions give cleaner uncritical citations.\
\cf7 31\cf11 \
\cf7 32\cf11 **2. Category B \'97 asserting the mismatch is the part that needs a claim-side judgment.** The cited paper's animal-only scope is fully metadata-grounded (MeSH `Animals`/`Mice`, no `Humans`). But whether a *citing claim* over-generalizes to humans depends on the claim's wording. Some citers correctly restrict to "in mice"; those are NOT mismatches. Only citers that drop the species qualifier or assert human/clinical relevance are true Category B cases. **Plan: extract the claim verbatim, then classify its asserted scope by explicit lexical markers ("in humans", "patients", "clinical") = text-grounded; anything requiring interpretation = model-inferred and flagged separately** \'97 exactly the metadata-grounded vs model-inferred split the project requires. This means Category B will carry more `model-inferred` flags than A or C, honestly marked.\
\cf7 33\cf11 \
\cf7 34\cf11 ## Recommendation\
\cf7 35\cf11 Proceed to full build with the staged candidates. No scope reduction needed. I'll (a) add the `citer_acknowledges_retraction` field to Category A, and (b) route Category B claim-scope classification through the explicit-marker vs inferred split. Awaiting your go-ahead.\
