\fs26 \cf7 \cb8 1\cf9 import\cf10  json\
\cf7 2\cf10 t = json.load(\cf11 open\cf10 (\cf11 "handoff/note_tables.json"\cf10 ))\
\cf7 3\cf10 A_tbl=\cf11 "\\n"\cf10 .join(t[\cf11 "A"\cf10 ]); B_tbl=\cf11 "\\n"\cf10 .join(t[\cf11 "B"\cf10 ]); C_tbl=\cf11 "\\n"\cf10 .join(t[\cf11 "C"\cf10 ])\
\cf7 4\cf10 \
\cf7 5\cf10 note = \cf11 f"""# Methodology \'97 Ground-Truth Citation-Verification Evaluation Set\
\cf7 6\cf11 \
\cf7 7\cf11 ## Purpose\
\cf7 8\cf11 A starter evaluation set of **17 cases** where every ground-truth label is checkable against structured metadata or an external record, requiring no subjective biological judgement to score. It supports three verification tasks and their metrics:\
\cf7 9\cf11 \
\cf7 10\cf11 - **Category A \'97 RETRACTED (7 cases):** the cited paper is retracted. Tests **retraction detection**.\
\cf7 11\cf11 - **Category B \'97 SCOPE_MISMATCH (5 cases):** the claim asserts a human/clinical finding but the cited paper is animal-only by MeSH. Tests **species-scope mismatch detection**.\
\cf7 12\cf11 - **Category C \'97 SUPPORTED (5 cases):** negative control \'97 cited paper not retracted, scope matches, claim consistent with the source. Should **pass clean**. Measures **false-positive rate / specificity**.\
\cf7 13\cf11 \
\cf7 14\cf11 Classes are roughly balanced (7 / 5 / 5) so per-class precision and recall are both meaningful.\
\cf7 15\cf11 \
\cf7 16\cf11 ## Data sources\
\cf7 17\cf11 1. **Retraction Watch database** (maintained by Crossref) \'97 downloaded from the Crossref Labs API (`api.labs.crossref.org/data/retractionwatch`), 70,871 records. Fields used: `RetractionNature`, `Reason`, `RetractionDate`, `OriginalPaperPubMedID`, `OriginalPaperDOI`. This is the ground-truth source for Category A retraction status.\
\cf7 18\cf11 2. **PubMed** (via the PubMed MCP connector) \'97 article metadata, primarily the **MeSH term list** (`mesh_terms`). MeSH organism check-tags (`Animals`, `Mice`, `Rats`, `Macaca mulatta`, `Zebrafish`, \'85) and population tags (`Adult`, `Aged`, `Middle Aged`, `Infant`, `Child`) are the ground-truth source for scope. Cited PubMed per connector terms of use; article DOIs are recorded in each case.\
\cf7 19\cf11 3. **OpenAlex** (via the literature MCP connector) \'97 the citation graph (`openalex_citations`, papers that cite a given work) to find real citing papers, and the `is_retracted` flag (used to confirm Category C sources are NOT retracted).\
\cf7 20\cf11 4. **NCBI E-utilities EFetch (PMC)** \'97 raw **JATS XML** full text of citing papers. Used instead of the connector's plain-text full text because JATS preserves inline `<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="\'85">` citation markers and the structured `<ref-list>`, which the plain-text rendering strips.\
\cf7 21\cf11 \
\cf7 22\cf11 ## How each case is built\
\cf7 23\cf11 1. **Pick a cited source** with a metadata-provable property (retracted per Retraction Watch; animal-only per MeSH; human + unretracted for controls).\
\cf7 24\cf11 2. **Find real citers** via `openalex_citations`, keep those with a **PMCID** (PMC open-access full text).\
\cf7 25\cf11 3. **Fetch the citer's JATS XML** from NCBI EFetch.\
\cf7 26\cf11 4. **Pin the citation by reference-id, not proximity.** Locate the `<ref>` in the citer whose `<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid|doi">` matches the cited source, giving its reference id (`rid`); then find every in-text `<xref rid="\'85">` pointing to that `rid`. **This makes the claim\uc0\u8594 cited-source link a structured-metadata match, not a text inference.**\
\cf7 27\cf11 5. **Extract the citing sentence verbatim.**\
\cf7 28\cf11 6. **Fabrication guard:** every stored claim is re-verified as a verbatim substring of the citer's whitespace-normalized full-text body. **All 17 cases pass** (`verbatim_verified=True`). Each row stores both `claim_text_verbatim` (exact, including inline `[n]` markers) and `claim_text` (same text with bracketed/parenthetical numeric citation markers removed for readability; no alphanumeric tokens \'97 e.g. gene names \'97 are ever altered).\
\cf7 29\cf11 \
\cf7 30\cf11 ## Ground-truth justification per category\
\cf7 31\cf11 - **Category A (RETRACTED):** justified directly by the Retraction Watch record (`RetractionNature='Retraction'`), with the verbatim `Reason` and `RetractionDate` carried in the row. **No inference** \'97 `provenance_label = metadata-grounded`, `inference_flag = False`.\
\cf7 32\cf11 - **Category B (SCOPE_MISMATCH):** the **cited paper's animal-only scope is metadata-grounded** \'97 the exact MeSH organism tags are recorded and `'Humans'` is absent. The **claim-side judgement** (whether the citing sentence over-generalizes the animal finding to humans/clinical use) is a **model reading of the claim wording** and is flagged `inference_flag = True`. Each row records the human/clinical scope markers the model relied on (`claim_scope_evidence`).\
\cf7 33\cf11 - **Category C (SUPPORTED):** **not-retracted** (OpenAlex `is_retracted=False`) and **human scope** (MeSH `Humans` + population tags) are metadata-grounded. That the claim is actually **supported** by the cited paper is a model judgement against the cited **abstract** and is flagged `inference_flag = True`.\
\cf7 34\cf11 \
\cf7 35\cf11 ## Metadata-grounded vs model-inferred split (honest accounting)\
\cf7 36\cf11 | Provenance | Count | Which part is grounded vs inferred |\
\cf7 37\cf11 |---|---|---|\
\cf7 38\cf11 | Fully metadata-grounded | 7 (all Category A) | Retraction status + citation linkage both from structured records |\
\cf7 39\cf11 | Has a model-inferred component | 10 (all B + C) | Cited-side fact (organism scope / retraction status) is metadata-grounded; the claim-side judgement (over-generalization / support) is model-inferred and flagged |\
\cf7 40\cf11 \
\cf7 41\cf11 **Nothing in Category A depends on model judgement.** In B and C, the axis your tool must ultimately decide (does the claim over-reach / is it supported) is inherently a claim-reading task; those rows isolate the metadata-grounded cited-side fact from the model-inferred claim-side judgement so the two never blur. Every B/C row carries an `inference_note` stating exactly which half is which.\
\cf7 42\cf11 \
\cf7 43\cf11 ## Per-case provenance summary\
\cf7 44\cf11 \
\cf7 45\cf11 ### Category A \'97 RETRACTED (retraction record is ground truth; all citers cite uncritically)\
\cf7 46\cf11 | case | cited PMID | cited title | retraction reason | retraction date | citer acknowledges retraction? | citing PMID |\
\cf7 47\cf11 |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\
\cf7 48\cf10 \{\cf12 \cb8 A_tbl\cf10 \cb8 \}\cf11 \
\cf7 49\cf11 \
\cf7 50\cf11 *Selection rule (per user):* claims were chosen to **rely on the specific retracted finding** (passing/methodological mentions dropped), and to be **uncritical** \'97 the citer treats the retracted result as valid (the hard case for a detector). `citer_acknowledges_retraction` is `no` for all 7. Two sources are recognizable (Anversa/SCIPIO cardiac stem cells, PMID 22088800; Macchiarini tracheobronchial transplant, PMID 22119609); five are lower-profile. Reasons span fabrication, unreliable data, image duplication/paper-mill, compromised peer review, plagiarism, and methods/results error.\
\cf7 51\cf11 \
\cf7 52\cf11 ### Category B \'97 SCOPE_MISMATCH (cited scope from MeSH; claim over-generalization model-judged)\
\cf7 53\cf11 | case | cited PMID | cited title | cited organism MeSH (Humans absent) | citing PMID |\
\cf7 54\cf11 |---|---|---|---|---|\
\cf7 55\cf10 \{\cf12 \cb8 B_tbl\cf10 \cb8 \}\cf11 \
\cf7 56\cf11 \
\cf7 57\cf11 ### Category C \'97 SUPPORTED control (not retracted + scope match, both metadata-grounded)\
\cf7 58\cf11 | case | cited PMID | cited title | cited population MeSH | citing PMID |\
\cf7 59\cf11 |---|---|---|---|---|\
\cf7 60\cf10 \{\cf12 \cb8 C_tbl\cf10 \cb8 \}\cf11 \
\cf7 61\cf11 \
\cf7 62\cf11 ## Confidence tiers\
\cf7 63\cf11 - **high** \'97 Category A: ground truth is a direct external-record lookup.\
\cf7 64\cf11 - **medium** \'97 Category B and C: cited-side fact is metadata-grounded, but the label also depends on a model reading of the claim (B: over-generalization; C: support), which a human annotator could occasionally score differently.\
\cf7 65\cf11 \
\cf7 66\cf11 ## Limitations (read before using)\
\cf7 67\cf11 1. **Claim-side scope/support in B and C is model-inferred**, not adjudicated against full text. For B, the tool's own job is to detect over-generalization; treat these as the intended target, but a strict reviewer may reclassify a borderline "general_unqualified" claim. For C, support is judged against the cited **abstract**, not the full paper \'97 a claim consistent with the abstract is a reasonable but not exhaustive SUPPORTED label.\
\cf7 68\cf11 2. **Dose and other unverifiable axes are not covered.** Per project rules, only axes provable from structured metadata (retraction, MeSH organism/population) are used as ground truth. Dose mismatch, statistical misuse, and outcome-switching are out of scope for this set.\
\cf7 69\cf11 3. **PMC open-access bias.** Citing papers are limited to those with PMC full text (~44% of examined citers yielded a usable verbatim claim in the pilot). This biases the citer sample toward open-access journals; it does not affect ground-truth correctness.\
\cf7 70\cf11 4. **Population-axis Category B was not populated.** All 5 B cases are the species axis (animal\uc0\u8594 human). A pediatric-vs-adult population mismatch case was scoped but not included in this starter set; the same MeSH-tag machinery (`Infant`/`Child` vs `Adult`) supports adding them.\
\cf7 71\cf11 5. **Two Category C cases share one cited source** (pembrolizumab trial, PMID 25891173) with distinct claims and distinct citers. Acceptable for a control set; broaden if you want strict source-uniqueness.\
\cf7 72\cf11 6. **Retraction timing.** Category A ground truth is current retraction status. Several claims were published before the cited paper was retracted (legitimately, at the time) \'97 this is realistic and is exactly what a retraction checker should still catch today. `retraction_date` is provided so you can model temporal logic if desired.\
\cf7 73\cf11 \
\cf7 74\cf11 ## Files\
\cf7 75\cf11 - `eval_set.csv` \'97 the 17 cases, flat table (one row per case).\
\cf7 76\cf11 - `eval_set.json` \'97 same cases with schema/label/provenance definitions for programmatic consumption.\
\cf7 77\cf11 - `candidate_sources.csv` \'97 the wider candidate pool the final cases were drawn from.\
\cf7 78\cf11 - `pilot_yield.md` \'97 the pre-build pilot yield report.\
\cf7 79\cf11 """\cf10 \
\cf7 80\cf11 open\cf10 (\cf11 "methodology.md"\cf10 ,\cf11 "w"\cf10 ).write(note)\
\cf7 81\cf9 print\cf10 (\cf11 "wrote methodology.md,"\cf10 , \cf11 len\cf10 (note), \cf11 "chars"\cf10 )\
\cf7 82
